AWS IAM & AWS Organizations
1. IAM Users
Types of Users
Root User
Admin User
Power User
Normal User

Root User: The root user is the AWS account owner and has full access to all AWS resources.

Admin User: An admin user generally has AdministratorAccess and can manage AWS resources and IAM.

Power User: A power user can manage most AWS services but normally cannot manage IAM users, groups, or policies.

Normal User: A user with limited permissions based on the IAM policies assigned to them.

2. IAM Groups

IAM Group: A collection of IAM users used to manage permissions for multiple users.

Developers Group
├── User A
├── User B
└── User C
Important Operations
Create IAM Group
Add users to IAM Group
Attach policies to IAM Group
3. IAM Policy Document

An IAM Policy Document is a JSON document that defines permissions for AWS resources.

Example:

{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": "s3:GetObject",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::my-bucket/*"
    }
  ]
}
Elements of a Policy Document
Effect → Allow or Deny
Action → What operation is allowed or denied
Resource → Which AWS resource
Condition (Optional) → Under what condition
Easy Way to Remember
Effect     → What decision?
Action     → What operation?
Resource   → On which resource?
Condition  → Under what condition?
4. Types of IAM Policies
1. AWS Managed Policy
Created and managed by AWS.
Provides commonly required permissions.
Can be attached to users, groups, or roles.
AWS manages updates to the policy.

Examples:

AdministratorAccess
AmazonS3ReadOnlyAccess
AmazonEC2FullAccess
2. Customer Managed Policy
Created and managed by the customer.
We define the required permissions.
Can be edited and versioned.
Can be reused across multiple users, groups, or roles.

Example:

A policy allowing only s3:GetObject on a specific S3 bucket.

{
  "Effect": "Allow",
  "Action": "s3:GetObject",
  "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::my-bucket/*"
}
3. Inline Policy
Created and embedded directly into one user, group, or role.
Has a one-to-one relationship with that identity.
Cannot be reused by another identity.
IAM Role
└── Inline Policy
    └── S3 Access
Easy Interview Answer
AWS Managed Policy
→ Managed by AWS

Customer Managed Policy
→ Created and managed by us

Inline Policy
→ Embedded directly into one IAM identity
5. IAM Authentication & Access
MFA for Root User

MFA provides an additional layer of security for the root account.

Password
   +
MFA Code
   ↓
AWS Account

MFA should be enabled for the root user.

MFA for IAM Users

IAM users can also use MFA for additional security when accessing AWS.

Access Key ID

Access Key ID is used to identify the AWS access credentials.

Secret Access Key

Secret Access Key is used with the Access Key ID to authenticate AWS CLI/API requests.

Access Key ID
      +
Secret Access Key
      ↓
AWS CLI / API

Never share or expose the Secret Access Key.

